---
title = "How to read a file"
meta_title = "How to read a file in Gleam"
subtitle = "Using the simplifile package"
description = "The simplifile package provides file system functionality that works with Gleam on both Erlang and JavaScript"
---

The `simplifile` package provides file-system functions that work both the
Erlang and JavaScript targets. Add it as a dependency to your package:

```cli
gleam add simplifile
```

Use the [`simplfile.read`](https://simplifile.hexdocs.pm/simplifile.html#read)
function to read the file contents to a string.

```gleam
import simplifile

pub fn main() -> Nil {
  case simplifile.read("path/to/file.txt") {
    Ok(contents) -> todo as "use file contents"
    Error(error) -> todo as "handle error"
  }
}
```

If the file contains binary data instead of human readable UTF8 text then you
can use the [`simplfile.read_bits`](https://simplifile.hexdocs.pm/simplifile.html#read_bits)
function, which reads the contents as a bit array.

```gleam
import simplifile

pub fn main() -> Nil {
  case simplifile.read_bits("photo.jpg") {
    Ok(jpg_data) -> todo as "use binary data"
    Error(error) -> todo as "handle error"
  }
}
```

If the file cannot be read for any reason, these functions will return a
[`simplifile.FileError`](https://simplifile.hexdocs.pm/simplifile.html#FileError).

::: alert-warning
### Files and web browsers

For security reasons web browsers do not let code directly read files, so if
your program runs in browser you will need to use a [HTML file input](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/input/file)
instead.

:::
